data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0451bfc8a946858f006a5178fe7eb88195bd45facf4e9015c3","logprobs":[],"output_index":0,"sequence_number":330,"text":"Azure AI Foundry Agent Service is Microsoft’s managed service for building, deploying, and operating AI agents in Azure AI Foundry. It’s designed to let developers create agents that can reason over instructions, use tools, connect to enterprise data, and coordinate multi-step tasks without having to assemble all the orchestration infrastructure themselves.\n\nIn practical terms, it gives you things like:\n\n- Hosted agent runtime\n- Tool calling / function orchestration\n- Integration with models in Azure AI Foundry\n- Connections to enterprise knowledge sources\n- Stateful conversations and agent threads\n- Security, governance, and Azure-native deployment controls\n\nTypical use cases include:\n\n- Internal copilots for employees\n- Customer support assistants\n- Agents that retrieve documents and answer questions\n- Workflow assistants that call APIs or backend functions\n- Multi-agent or multi-step business process automation\n\nA simple way to think about it:\n\n- Azure AI Foundry gives you the broader platform for AI app development.\n- Foundry Agent Service is the part focused on agentic applications: assistants that can plan, retrieve, call tools, and act.\n\nCommon capabilities often associated with agent services include:\n\n- Instructions/persona setup\n- Conversation memory\n- Retrieval over indexed files/data\n- Function/tool invocation\n- Observability and evaluation\n- Identity, access control, and compliance features through Azure\n\nIf you want, I can also give you one of these:\n\n1. a plain-English explanation,\n2. a technical architecture overview,\n3. a comparison with Azure OpenAI Assistants / OpenAI Assistants,\n4. sample Python or C# code,\n5. pricing and availability info."}

